---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import Measure from "../components/Measure.astro";
import Stamped from "../components/Stamped.astro";
import DurationChart from "../components/DurationChart.astro";
import Quote from "../components/Quote.astro";
import facts from "../generated/facts.json";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const loop = facts.recorded.edit_loop;
const bin = facts.recorded.hello_binary;
const lib = facts.recorded.library_size;
const front = facts.recorded.front_end;
const pack = facts.recorded.context_pack;
const runtime = facts.recorded.runtime;

const ratios = loop.ratios as number[];
const loopClaim = `About ${loop.claim.value}x less than Crystal, on the line you just changed.`;
const loopBand = `${Math.min(...ratios).toFixed(2)}x to ${Math.max(...ratios).toFixed(2)}x across ${loop.sessions.length} published sessions.`;

const massRatio = bin.crystal.kb / bin.iyi.kb;
const crystalSide = 216;
const iyiSide = crystalSide / Math.sqrt(massRatio);
const startRatio = (bin.crystal.ms / bin.iyi.ms).toFixed(1);

const stringRow = runtime.rows.find((r) => r.workload === "string building")!;

const packClaim = `${pack.tokens_low} to ${pack.tokens_high}% fewer prompt tokens, with no extra rounds.`;
const packBand = `The grounding pack is ${pack.size_low} to ${pack.size_high}% of the size of the sources it replaces, over ${pack.runs} measured runs.`;

const targets = [
  "x86_64-linux-gnu",
  "x86_64-linux-musl",
  "aarch64-linux-gnu",
  "arm-linux-gnueabihf",
  "x86_64-darwin",
  "aarch64-darwin",
  "x86_64-w64-mingw32",
  "x86_64-windows-msvc",
  "wasm32-wasi",
];
const ran = new Set(["x86_64-linux-gnu", "x86_64-linux-musl", "aarch64-linux-gnu", "wasm32-wasi"]);
---

<Page
  title="Why iyi"
  summary="iyi runs your Crystal code today and rebuilds it about 9x faster. Developer experience, AI experience, portability, performance and efficiency: four things, one rule, every number measured."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">Why iyi</p>
      <h1>Your Crystal code, rebuilt {loop.claim.value}x faster, with a compiler your agents can talk to.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        iyi is one design decision seen from four sides. A module compiles
        against its dependencies' <strong>declarations</strong>, never their
        bodies. So a build reads less, a program carries less, a tool can read
        an interface without a repository, and a person waits less. Every
        number on this page was measured, and the machine and command are
        printed beside it.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="cta" href={`${base}playground/`}>Take the tour</a>
        <a class="cta ghost" href={`${base}#crystal`}>Switch from Crystal</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">Two things first</p>
      <div class="two">
        <div>
          <h2>It runs your Crystal code. Today.</h2>
          <p>
            iyi's compiler is Crystal's compiler. Your <code>.cr</code> files
            build unchanged, <code>iyi build --crystal</code> gives an iyi
            program Crystal's standard library and <code>require</code>, and the
            shards you already use come with it. Kemal, db, ameba, sqlite3: they
            build and they run. There is no port to do first.
          </p>
          <p><a href={`${base}#crystal`}>See the Kemal server, quoted from the README</a></p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2>It compiles faster. Measured.</h2>
          <p>
            Change one line in a {facts.structural.generated.toLocaleString("en-GB")}
            line project and rebuild. iyi reads the declarations of the other
            twenty-nine modules and none of their bodies, so the line you just
            changed costs about {loop.claim.value}x less than under Crystal's
            rules, on the same compiler binary. The bars below run at real speed.
          </p>
          <p><a href="#dx">The measurement, with its machine</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="four">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">The four</p>
      <h2>One rule, four sides.</h2>
      <ol class="pillars">
        <li><a href="#dx"><span>1</span> Developer experience</a></li>
        <li><a href="#aix"><span>2</span> AI experience</a></li>
        <li><a href="#portability"><span>3</span> Portability</a></li>
        <li><a href="#performance"><span>4</span> Performance and efficiency</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 1. DX ------------------------------------------------------------- */}
  <section class="pillar" id="dx">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">1. Developer experience</p>
      <h2>The loop you are actually in is the loop that got fast.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        Nobody uses a language through full builds. You change a line, you
        build, you look. That loop is what R-1 was written for, and it is the
        number this project exists for.
      </p>

      <Stamped
        claim={loopClaim}
        band={loopBand}
        subject={loop.subject}
        machine={loop.machine}
        command={loop.command}
        outcome="win"
      >
        <DurationChart />
      </Stamped>

      <ul class="points">
        <li>
          <strong>Errors name the rule, and the fix.</strong> A refusal says
          which rule it enforces and writes out the line you meant. The
          compiler teaches the language at the moment you break it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A framework's promise is checked at your line.</strong> Below,
          a Kemal port refuses a handler that returns something a body cannot
          be made of, by a module that has never heard of your types. Kemal
          itself accepts the same block and serves an empty body forever.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The tools are the compiler.</strong> <code>iyi lsp</code>{" "}
          answers completion, references, rename, hover and diagnostics from
          the same front end a build uses, so a build, <code>iyi check</code>
          and your editor cannot disagree about what is clean.{" "}
          <code>iyi tool format</code> knows the syntax. <code>iyi mod dump</code>{" "}
          prints a compiled module as text.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <Quote id="bad_body" as="diagnostic" caption="A framework's contract, enforced at the caller" />
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 2. AIX ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
  <section class="pillar aix" id="aix">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">2. AI experience</p>
      <h2>A compiler built to be an agent's tool, not wrapped as one.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        An agent needs two things from a language: a boundary it can read, and
        a loop it can afford. Most languages give it neither, so it reads whole
        repositories into a prompt and hopes. iyi gives it both, for the same
        reason a person gets them: a module's interface is a <em>file</em>, not
        a convention.
      </p>

      <div class="aix-grid">
        <div class="aix-copy">
          <h3>The interface is a file</h3>
          <p>
            A <code>.iyimod</code> holds a module's declarations, with every
            type written down, and its machine code. A tool can read exactly
            what a module offers without the repository that produced it, and
            check a change against it without building the world. That is not
            an agent feature bolted on. It is R-1 and R-2, asked a question.
          </p>
          <h3>"Did my change reach anybody?" has an answer</h3>
          <p>
            <code>iyi mod diff</code> compares two artifacts and says whether
            the <em>interface</em> moved, which consumers have to be rebuilt,
            and which declarations are gone or new. Rename a local and it says
            the interface is unchanged. <code>--exit-code</code> makes that a
            branch in a script, or in an agent's plan.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <Quote id="mod_diff" as="console" caption="what moved, and who it reaches" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="loop-head">The loop, one verb per step</h3>
      <ol class="loop">
        <li>
          <span class="verb">iyi mod context FILE</span>
          <span class="what">
            <strong>Ground.</strong> Prints what an edit to this file needs to
            know: every import's exact surface, nothing else. Cut it further
            with <code>--budget N</code>, by a stated ladder rather than by
            truncation.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="verb">iyi check -f json</span>
          <span class="what">
            <strong>Check.</strong> The front end's verdict as data: file,
            line, column, the rule, and a <code>suggested_edit</code> span the
            compiler already computed. No binary is built. The exit code is the
            answer.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="verb">iyi fix</span>
          <span class="what">
            <strong>Fix.</strong> Applies exactly those edits, one per round,
            recompiling between, until clean or until an error carries no
            edit. Nothing is invented.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="verb">iyi test --affected FILE</span>
          <span class="what">
            <strong>Test.</strong> Runs only the tests whose import closure
            reaches the change, decided in milliseconds and exactly.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="verb">iyi run --sandbox</span>
          <span class="what">
            <strong>Run, safely.</strong> Cross-compiles to wasm32-wasi and
            runs under a host that preopens nothing: no filesystem, no
            network, no environment. Generated code executes against a
            runtime holding nothing.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="verb">iyi mcp · iyi lsp</span>
          <span class="what">
            <strong>Serve.</strong> The same verbs over the Model Context
            Protocol for agents and the Language Server Protocol for editors.
            Each is a shell around the same binary, so what a harness gets over
            the wire is byte for byte what a shell would have printed. No agent
            mode, no forked behaviour.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <Stamped
        claim={packClaim}
        band={packBand}
        subject={pack.subject}
        machine={pack.machine}
        command={pack.command}
        outcome="win"
      >
        <p class="stamp-body">
          A model writing against the grounding pack instead of the sources
          spent {pack.tokens_low} to {pack.tokens_high}% fewer prompt tokens
          and needed no more rounds to get there. Counted, not timed, so there
          is no machine to name. The whole loop is gated in CI by{" "}
          <code>bench/agent_loop.py</code>, ground to check to fix to affected
          tests to MCP to sandbox, beside an LSP session.
        </p>
      </Stamped>

      <p class="sub">
        Said plainly, as the README says it: the mechanism is built and the
        claim is young. If the rules turn out not to be enough for agents,
        that is a thing to measure rather than to promise.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 3. Portability --------------------------------------------------- */}
  <section class="pillar" id="portability">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">3. Portability</p>
      <h2>Compiles for <Measure of="targets" />. Runs on four, every build.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        The object is cross-compiled here and linked there with the target's
        own toolchain, which is the command <code>--cross-compile</code>{" "}
        prints. CI type-checks the library for all <Measure of="targets" />
        every push, and four of them are <em>run</em>: the check is that they
        print what the same program printed on the machine that compiled them.
      </p>
      <ul class="targets">
        {
          targets.map((t) => (
            <li class={ran.has(t) ? "ran" : undefined}>
              <code>{t}</code>
              {ran.has(t) && <span>run every build</span>}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="sub">
        The wasm32 build is one self-contained module, which is why every
        program on the <a href={`${base}playground/`}>tour</a> runs in your
        browser. Darwin is "the code generator has no objection" and needs a
        runner CI does not have; Windows compiles and links, and what it prints
        at run time cannot be trusted yet. Both are said here rather than left
        to be found.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 4. Performance & efficiency ---------------------------------------- */}
  <section class="pillar" id="performance">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">4. Performance and efficiency</p>
      <h2>Native code, a small program, and a front end that answers before you notice.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        iyi generates native code through LLVM. At run time the two libraries
        are within noise where they do the same work. What changes is what a
        program <em>carries</em>: it links only what it uses, and iyi's own
        library is <Measure of="prelude" /> lines against Crystal's{" "}
        {lib.crystal.toLocaleString("en-GB")}, <Measure of="prelude_kb" unit="KB" />{" "}
        on disk beside the binary.
      </p>

      <div class="two">
        <Stamped
          claim={`${Math.round(massRatio)}x smaller, and starts ${startRatio}x sooner.`}
          subject={bin.subject}
          machine={bin.machine}
          command={bin.command}
          outcome="win"
        >
          <div class="mass">
            <figure class="mass-item">
              <div class="square iyi" style={`width:${iyiSide}px;height:${iyiSide}px`}></div>
              <figcaption>
                <span class="mass-name">iyi</span>
                <span class="mass-val">{bin.iyi.kb} KB</span>
                <span class="mass-val dim">starts in {bin.iyi.ms} ms</span>
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="mass-item">
              <div class="square crystal" style={`width:${crystalSide}px;height:${crystalSide}px`}></div>
              <figcaption>
                <span class="mass-name">Crystal's library</span>
                <span class="mass-val">{bin.crystal.kb.toLocaleString("en-GB")} KB</span>
                <span class="mass-val dim">starts in {bin.crystal.ms} ms</span>
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
          <p class="mass-caption">
            The same <code>puts "hello"</code>, drawn at true area. A difference
            this large rendered as length would flatter it.
          </p>
        </Stamped>

        <Stamped
          claim={`The front end answers hello in ${front.seconds} s.`}
          subject={front.subject}
          machine={front.machine}
          command={front.command}
          outcome="win"
        >
          <p class="stamp-body">
            Parse, check, and refuse or accept, before any code is generated.
            That is what <code>iyi check</code> and the language server pay per
            question, and it is why an editor and an agent can ask the compiler
            on every keystroke.
          </p>
        </Stamped>
      </div>

      <h3 class="loses-head">Where it loses, said here</h3>
      <Stamped
        claim={`${stringRow.gc_off.toFixed(2)}x slower at building strings, with the collector off.`}
        subject={runtime.subject}
        machine={runtime.machine}
        command={runtime.command}
        outcome="loss"
      >
        <p class="stamp-body">
          As it runs, the same workload is {stringRow.as_it_runs.toFixed(2)}x,
          within noise. The collector is masking a slower builder, and the
          README says so rather than publishing the flattering column. iyi is
          also not quick at compiling a lot of code it has never seen; it is
          quick on the loop, which is the row that matters.
        </p>
      </Stamped>
      <p class="sub">
        Young parts, named: iyi's own prelude carries the small-tool floor and
        no sockets, TLS or serialisation yet; tasks run on Linux and macOS
        arm64 only; there is no package registry, and <code>--crystal</code>{" "}
        still leans on shards. Every one of these is a line in the README, not
        a footnote.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band closing">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Check it yourself.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        The tour runs in your browser. The lessons run real programs from the
        repository. The spec records what was measured and what was thrown
        away. And every number above names the command that prints it.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="cta" href={`${base}playground/`}>Take the tour</a>
        <a class="cta ghost" href={`${base}learn/`}>Learn the rule</a>
        <a class="cta ghost" href="https://github.com/sdogruyol/iyi">Source on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    section {
      padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4.5rem);
    }

    .hero {
      padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem) clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    }

    .hero h1 {
      font-size: var(--step-5);
      font-weight: 800;
      letter-spacing: -0.03em;
      max-width: 22ch;
      margin-block: 0.5rem 1rem;
    }

    .lede {
      font-size: var(--step-1);
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: var(--graphite);
      max-width: 60ch;
      margin: 0 0 1.6rem;
    }

    .hero-cta {
      display: flex;
      gap: 0.8rem;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }

    .band {
      background: var(--raise);
      border-block: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
    }

    .band h2,
    .pillar h2,
    .four h2 {
      font-size: var(--step-3);
      margin-block: 0.4rem 0.8rem;
      max-width: 26ch;
    }

    .band p,
    .sub {
      color: var(--graphite);
      max-width: 62ch;
    }

    .two {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 22rem), 1fr));
      gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .two :global(.stamped) {
      margin: 0;
    }

    .pillars {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 14rem), 1fr));
      gap: 0.75rem;
    }

    .pillars a {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.8rem;
      padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
      border: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      color: var(--ink);
      font-weight: 600;
    }

    .pillars a:hover {
      border-color: var(--graphite);
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    .pillars span {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-weight: 800;
      color: var(--brand);
      font-size: var(--step-1);
    }

    .pillar {
      border-block-start: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
      scroll-margin-top: 1rem;
    }

    .pillar .label {
      color: var(--brand-deep);
    }

    .points {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 1.6rem 0;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      gap: 0.9rem;
      max-width: 62ch;
    }

    .points li {
      position: relative;
      padding-inline-start: 1.6rem;
      color: var(--graphite);
    }

    .points li::before {
      content: "";
      position: absolute;
      left: 0.1rem;
      top: 0.55em;
      width: 0.5rem;
      height: 0.5rem;
      border-radius: 50%;
      background: var(--brand);
    }

    .points strong {
      color: var(--ink);
    }

    /* AIX ------------------------------------------------------------- */

    .aix {
      background: var(--brand-soft);
    }

    .aix h2 {
      max-width: 24ch;
    }

    .aix-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.2fr);
      gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem);
      align-items: start;
      margin-block: 1.5rem 2.5rem;
    }

    @media (max-width: 52rem) {
      .aix-grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }
    }

    .aix-copy h3 {
      font-size: var(--step-1);
      margin-block: 0.8rem 0.4rem;
    }

    .aix-copy p {
      color: var(--graphite);
    }

    .loop-head,
    .loses-head {
      font-size: var(--step-2);
      margin-block: 2rem 1rem;
    }

    .loop {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0 0 2rem;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      gap: 0.6rem;
      counter-reset: step;
    }

    .loop li {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 15rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 1.25rem;
      padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
      background: var(--paper);
      border: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      align-items: start;
      counter-increment: step;
    }

    @media (max-width: 40rem) {
      .loop li {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }
    }

    .verb {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-weight: 600;
      color: var(--brand-deep);
      font-size: var(--step--1);
    }

    .verb::before {
      content: counter(step) " ";
      color: var(--mute);
      font-weight: 400;
    }

    .what {
      color: var(--graphite);
      font-size: var(--step--1);
    }

    .what strong {
      color: var(--ink);
    }

    .stamp-body {
      margin: 0;
      color: var(--graphite);
      font-size: var(--step--1);
      max-width: 60ch;
    }

    /* Targets --------------------------------------------------------- */

    .targets {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 1.4rem 0;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 16rem), 1fr));
      gap: 0.5rem;
    }

    .targets li {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: 0.6rem;
      align-items: center;
      padding: 0.55rem 0.9rem;
      border: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      font-size: var(--step--1);
    }

    .targets li.ran {
      border-color: var(--brand);
    }

    .targets code {
      background: none;
      border: 0;
      padding: 0;
    }

    .targets span {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--step--2);
      color: var(--brand-deep);
      white-space: nowrap;
    }

    /* Mass ------------------------------------------------------------ */

    .mass {
      display: flex;
      gap: 2rem;
      align-items: flex-end;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      margin-block: 0.5rem 0.8rem;
    }

    .mass-item {
      margin: 0;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.5rem;
      align-items: flex-start;
    }

    .square.iyi {
      background: var(--brand);
      min-width: 6px;
      min-height: 6px;
    }

    .square.crystal {
      border: var(--hair) solid var(--hairline);
      background: repeating-linear-gradient(
        45deg,
        transparent 0 4px,
        var(--hairline) 4px 5px
      );
    }

    .mass-item figcaption {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--step--2);
    }

    .mass-name {
      color: var(--mute);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    }

    .mass-val {
      font-weight: 600;
    }

    .mass-val.dim {
      font-weight: 400;
      color: var(--graphite);
    }

    .mass-caption {
      margin: 0;
      color: var(--mute);
      font-size: var(--step--2);
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
    }

    .closing h2 {
      margin-block-end: 0.5rem;
    }
  </style>
</Page>
